-- Ballast — the fund's holdings, enumerated on the ledger.
--
-- Daml cannot ask "what does party X own?". A choice can only look at contracts it is handed.
-- So while `PublishNav` took a list of holdings from the manager, the manager could hand it a
-- SUBSET — understating the fund, depressing NAV per unit, and issuing cheap units to itself.
-- Every individual check passed; the set was the lie.
--
-- The VaultIndex closes that. It is the single enumeration of what the fund owns, and it is
-- the only thing NAV and policy validation will read.
--
-- ┌─ WHY THE INDEX IS COMPLETE, rather than merely asserted ───────────────────────────────┐
-- │ It is not trusted — it is inductively maintained:                                      │
-- │   1. The manager cannot move fund assets outside a choice, because fund holdings are    │
-- │      signed by (registry, vault) and the manager holds ReadAs on the vault, not         │
-- │      CanActAs. See the security model in Ballast.Mandate.                               │
-- │   2. Every choice that moves assets updates this index in the same transaction.         │
-- │   3. Therefore no asset can enter or leave the fund without the index changing with it. │
-- │ Completeness follows from the party rule. If that rule is ever relaxed, this guarantee  │
-- │ is the first thing to fall.                                                             │
-- └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
module Ballast.Vault where

import Splice.Api.Token.HoldingV1

import Ballast.Valuation

template VaultIndex
  with
    manager  : Party
    vault    : Party
    fundId   : Text
    holdings : [ContractId Holding]
  where
    signatory manager, vault

-- | The fund's whole portfolio, read through the index.
--
-- A stale entry — a holding archived out from under the index — makes `fetch` fail and the
-- whole transaction abort. That is the correct direction to fail: a NAV computed from an
-- index that no longer matches reality is worse than no NAV at all.
portfolioOf : ContractId VaultIndex -> Party -> Text -> Update [HoldingView]
portfolioOf idxCid vault fundId = do
  idx <- fetch idxCid
  assertMsg "vault index belongs to a different vault" (idx.vault == vault)
  assertMsg "vault index belongs to a different fund" (idx.fundId == fundId)
  vaultHoldings vault idx.holdings
